681:
As used in this section, "inoperable motor vehicle," shall mean any contrivance or part thereof, propelled by power and used in the transportation of persons or property on public streets and highways, the condition of which is one or more of the following:
1. Wrecked;
2. Dismantled;
3. Partially dismantled;
4. Inoperative;
5. Abandoned;
6. Discarded.
(Ord. of 10-8-2019(1), § 1)

713:
A. When utility service is discontinued either at the request of the consumer or due to nonpayment, the security deposit on file will be applied to the balance (if any) on the account and the remainder of the deposit (if any) shall be refunded by check and payable to the individual whose name appears on the utility account.
B. In the event the security deposit is insufficient to cover the balance on the account and the customer does not remit payment to cover said amount, the individual whose name is on the account is subject to referral to a collection agency. Said individual shall be responsible for the amount due on the account as well as any addition charges that may be imposed by the collection agency. Additional charges may include but are not limited to, postage, second notices, collection fees, court processing fees and attorney fees.
C. The city reserves the right to discontinue its service without notice for the following additional reasons:
1. To prevent fraud or abuse;
2. The consumer's willful disregard of the city's rules;
3. Emergency repairs;
4. Insufficiency of supply due to circumstances beyond the city's control;
5. Legal processes;
6. Direction of public authorities;
7. Strike, riot, fire, flood, unavoidable accident.
(Prior Code, § 37-014)
